son diary", "role": "hint", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-23T04:48:29Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "😂😂😂😂😂😂😂😂😂😂", "role": "attempt", "is_correct": false, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-23T04:48:33Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Lallu saale", "role": "attempt", "is_correct": true, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-23T04:49:04Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Wo khud bolte the kabhi kabhi toh “lallu ka tempo high hai”", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-23T04:49:07Z", "username": "Abhishek Vats", "text": "9. Bhagat Singh was an avid reader of X’s most famous novel, The Jungle. Singh was particularly inspired by X’s critique of how religion is often used by the ruling class to keep the poor submissive. In his Jail Notebook, Singh famously referenced (and slightly paraphrased) a concept from X:\n\"Yes: It was perhaps X that wrote at some place that just make a man a believer in immortality and then rob him of all his riches and possessions. He will bear it with perfect good humour.\"\nThis thought was central to Singh’s essay \"Why I Am an Atheist.\" He used X’s logic to argue that the promise of a \"better afterlife\" was a psychological tool used to make the oppressed accept their misery in the present world.", "role": "confirmation", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-23T04:49:25Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Oh", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-23T04:49:35Z", "username": "Kartikey Pradhan", "text": "Bakunin?", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timestamp": "2026-03-23T04:49:40Z", "username": "Pavan Pamidimarri", "text": "sinclair ‎", "role": "chat", "is_correct": null, "has_media": false, "media": null}, {"timest